<div class="text">

<b>HOW DO I JOIN COU?<br>
</b>We're flattered that you would like to join our ranks.&nbsp; Becoming a part 
of Clan of Unity is not difficult, but does require some effort and commitment 
on your part.&nbsp; First, our recruiting works on a vouching basis.&nbsp; After 
grouping with several of our members, ask those you've grouped with the most to 
vouch for you, and propose your membership by posting on our Forum.&nbsp; Make a 
new post with your main character's name as the subject in the &quot;New Members&quot; 
section of the forum.&nbsp; Ask your friends to post on your behalf there.&nbsp; 
If you receive five replies in the affirmative, and no one objects, you will be 
invited by an officer at their earliest convenience.<br>
<br>
<b>WHAT HAPPENS IF I TURN OUT TO BE A JERK?<br>
</b>Bill and Ted said it best.&nbsp; &quot;Be excellent to each other.&quot;&nbsp; If 
you're not, we don't want ya.&nbsp; If you are a problem, you will be phased out 
of CoU.&nbsp; The first time you cause a problem, as determined by a committee 
of officers, you will be issued a warning.&nbsp; Once you have been warned, 
repeat problems will be dealt with using scaling suspensions.&nbsp;&nbsp; You 
will initially be suspended for one week, with a week added for each additional 
problem.&nbsp; So, for example, let's say it's the third time you absolutely, 
positively had to be obnoxious.&nbsp; The first time, you got a warning, the 
second time, you got a week.&nbsp; This time, you'll be suspended for two weeks.&nbsp; 
The idea behind such a severe punishment scale is to make sure everyone's 
treating everyone else how they should at all times.&nbsp; If you believe you 
have been suspended/warned unfairly, send mail to
<a href="mailto:grromp@aol.com">grromp@aol.com</a> and the matter will be 
investigated.<br>
<br>
<b>HOW DO RAIDS WORK?<br>
</b>Our raiding policy is focused more on fairness than on loot.&nbsp; We 
realize that people have lives outside of EQ, and we do not require attendance 
on any raids.&nbsp; We do, however require that you attend five raids before you 
roll on any loot, and you may request assistance on a personal raid (epic or 
quest) only after you have attended a number of raids equal to the number of 
people you will require.&nbsp; For example, if an epic fight you need requires 
24 people, you must attend 24 raids before requesting guild assistance with this 
raid.&nbsp; After that raid, if you need a ring battle which requires 18 people, 
you have to attend another 18 raids before requesting assistance with this.&nbsp; 
Exceptions may be made at our officers' discretion, so please talk to one if you 
have any questions.&nbsp; The intent of this rule is to make sure that every CoU 
member is putting in as much to the guild as they take out.</div>

